<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trustees for the Establishment of the Colony of Georgia in America</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1732–1752</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Trustees for the Establishment of the Colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in America were the board, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-oglethorpe">James Oglethorpe</a>, that received a royal charter from George II in 1732 to found and govern Georgia. They aimed to settle the deserving poor and to shield <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a> from Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, and they banned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, hard liquor and large estates. Facing settler opposition, they lifted the bans and surrendered their charter to the Crown in 1752.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trustees-for-the-establishment-of-the-colony-of-georgia-in-america.jpg" alt="Trustees for the Establishment of the Colony of Georgia in America" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: William Verelst · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Audience_Given_by_the_Trustees_of_Georgia_to_a_Delegation_of_Creek_Indians.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
